Thanks, Janis.

I could have saved _so_ much writing if I'd known
that was there.

It has almost word for word some of what I just
posted.

It should be more emphasized on that page that the
programmer using overlays gets to carefully _design_
overlays to contain exactly what in virtual systems
is called a "working set" of (swap pages containing)
functionalities that need to run together, rather
than depend on the scattering of the usual compiler
and linkage edit system to "accidentally" come up
with enough co-running functionality co-located in
sufficiently few swap pages that a working set of
swap pages can be had all at once in available real
memory rather than memory thrashing occurring.

A very difficult issue with overlays is where you
put the data that needs to be passed from one
overlay to the next, since the new overlay has to
arrive tabula rasa w.r.t. that data but still
knowing where the data it needs will be found.

